window.__saqPaperQuestions("2017-2-metro-north",[{"number":1,"title":"","stemText":"Booklet one:\nSAQ 1-‐9\nBooklet two:\nSAQ 10-‐18\nBooklet three: SAQ 19-‐27 Props Booklet: All props\nSAQ 1 (9 Minutes)\n(Total 18 marks) A 20 year old male patient starts talking to you about anaphylaxis, whilst he waits for the result of an x-‐ray for a twisted ankle. He is surprised when you tell him that people can actually die from anaphylaxis.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What are 4 recognised possible risk factors for a fatal anaphylactic reaction?\n(4 Marks) Now his curiosity is piqued, as he is allergic to pecan nuts. He says he can often first tell from his skin that he is getting an anaphylactic reaction."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 3 skin manifestations consistent with an underlying anaphylactic\nreaction. (3 marks) You then ask him if he carries an EpiPen, and he says he used to, but has now forgotten how to use it. You decide to tell him exactly how to use it"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe five (5) steps on exactly how a patient uses an EpiPen. (5 Marks) Undeterred, he says he has heard that “adrenaline is a really dangerous drug”, and should\n• nly ever be used by an expert. You correct him by saying that it is life-‐saving and\nabsolutely essential in anaphylaxis, but agree there are some side effects and dangers, particularly if used incorrectly."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List Three (3) recognised ‘normal’ side effects of being given adrenaline. (3 Marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List three (3) serious / potentially lethal side effects of being given adrenaline too fast, too concentrated or too much? (3 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>11/18</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Underlying asthma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Other atopic disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Delay to, or no use of adrenaline</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Upright posture during shock</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiorespiratory disease in general (irrespective of age, and including COPD /</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IHD etc)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Misdiagnosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Carelessness with deliberate exposure / ingestion (of a known allergen)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Carelessness with iatrogenic drug exposure (to a known drug allergy)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">[ <em><strong>https://www.uptodate.com/contents/fatal-anaphylaxis</strong></em><em><strong>. Table 1</strong></em>]</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Itch (pruritus)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Erythema (flushing)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Urticaria (hives / wheals)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Angioedema (swelling)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pallor (in shock)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">[ <em><strong>https://www.uptodate.com/contents/anaphylaxis-symptoms-and-diagnosis-</strong></em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em><strong>beyond-the-basics</strong></em>]</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Store the EpiPen at room temperature</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Use the EpiPen as soon as you recognise you are having an anaphylactic reaction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Flip open the yellow cap (green if EpiPen Junior) carrier tube</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Grip in your hand with orange tip (needle end) pointing downward (ready to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">inject)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Remove / pull off the blue safety cap on the other end of the EpiPen</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Place EpiPen orange tip against your upper, outer thigh (must say thigh, not arm)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Push the EpiPen inwards firmly, until you hear a ‘click’</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hold in place for 3 seconds after adrenaline delivery (needle pops out with the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">click)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Remove EpiPen and massage injection area for 10 seconds</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Make certain someone else (or you if alone) has called an Ambulance at the same</div><div class=\"answer-line\">time ie. ‘call for help’</div><div class=\"answer-line\">[ <em><strong>https://www.epipen.com/en/prescribing-information#Patient</strong></em> ]</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Nausea</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tremor</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Palpitations</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Anxiety</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Headache</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Acute hypertension  / hypertensive crisis (one mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Myocardial ischaemia / myocardial infarction (one mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ventricular tachycardia / VF (one mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intracranial haemorrhage</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Acute pulmonary oedema</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Peripheral ischaemia / gangrene (one mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":2,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 Marks)\nAn 11 month old boy is brought in by ambulance following a brief 1-‐2min generalised\ntonic clonic seizure. He was well prior to the onset of seizure and has no previous history\n• f seizures.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List six (6) possible causes for the seizure other than a febrile convulsion: (6 Marks) On further assessment the child has a temperature of 38.5 and evidence of a viral upper respiratory tract infection.\nThe child is GCS 15 with no focal neurology. You are comfortable that the child has had a\nfebrile convulsion."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List three (3) criteria for the diagnosis of a simple febrile convulsion. (3 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"It is deemed safe for discharge. State three (3) criteria that suggest this patient is safe to be discharged home. (3 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Meningitis/encephalitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Hypoglycaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Trauma including NAI</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Electrolyte disturbance</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Cardiac arrhythmia (Prolonged QT)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Inborn error of metabolism</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> New onset epilepsy/epilepsy syndrome including infantile spasms</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> &lt;15mins</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Generalised</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Full recovery &lt;1 hr</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> No recurrence within 24hrs</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Good social supports/parent willing and able</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Reasonable proximity to hospital</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Full neurological recovery</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Normal Obs (Except Temp)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> GP follow up arranged</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Counselling/safety netting + advise leaflet provided</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Well hydrated with ongoing good fluid intake</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Source of fever identified with appropriate advice given</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":3,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 marks)\nA 67 year old lady has been brought to your emergency department with spontaneous\nbilateral epistaxis. This started about 2 hours ago while she was in her backyard gardening. She is complaining of nausea and difficulty breathing. There is no history of trauma. She has a background of atrial fibrillation, ischaemic heart disease and hypertension. The ambulance officers have estimated about 500 mls of blood loss. Her medications include:\nRivaroxaban 20 mg PO OD\nDigoxin 125 mcg PO OD\nMetoprolol 50 mg PO BD\nPerindopril 5mg PO OD\nHer observations are:\n\nTemp\n36.0 degrees\n\nBP\n150/100 mmHg Pulse 120 bpm O2 Sats. 91% RA\n\nRR\n25 breaths/min\n\nGCS\n15/15 Your registrar has identified that she has ongoing active bleeding and is becoming more distressed.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List five (5) immediate management options you would perform for this patient. (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List and justify 3 important investigations that would assist you in the\nresuscitation of this patient. Investigation Justification You have been unsuccessful in your approach to haemostasis. You suspect a posterior source for the bleeding."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List three (3) options for specific management of posterior epistaxis. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Commence appropriate first aid - Sit upright, Ice, Local pressure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Resuscitation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Fluids/Blood</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. Oxygen with end-points</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Assess and institute specific management - Anterior vs Posterior, Escalating</div><div class=\"answer-line\">approach to haemorrhage control</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Blood pressure lowering - Diastolic &lt;90 mmHg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Consider reversing anticoagulation - Tranexamic acid/Prothrombin complex</div><div class=\"answer-line\">concentrate/Idarucixumab/Haemodialysis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>II.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Blood gas for pH, Haemoglobin, Lactate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Group and Hold - Likely need for blood transfusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. ECG - Significant tachycardia in context of known atrial fibrillation and ischaemic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">heart disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Chest XRAY - Dyspnoea, Low O2 Saturations, Possible Aspiration</div><div class=\"answer-line\">You have been unsuccessful in your approach to haemostasis. You suspect a posterior</div><div class=\"answer-line\">source for the bleeding.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>III.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Posterior cauterisation under general anaesthesia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Arterial ligation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Embolisation via femoral approach by interventional radiology</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Local injection with lignocaine/adrenaline around sphenopalatine artery</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":4,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 Marks) A 16 year old male patient presents to your ED with a laceration to the plantar surface of the heel of his L foot sustained whilst walking in a freshwater creek bed. The wound was heavily soiled with mud and has been superficially cleaned on arrival to ED. There is no visible foreign body on X-‐ray. He has no known allergies and is unvaccinated. Your junior doctor has attempted to infiltrate with local anaesthetic but the skin is very thick and this has been unsuccessful. You decide to perform regional anaesthesia to allow washout and laceration repair. (See props booklet for photo please)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List the type of block and the landmarks you would use to perform a regional nerve block for this patient? Name of block: Landmarks:"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List the drug and dose you would use for the regional block for this patient. (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State the details of your preferred method of wound closure in this patient. (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List 4 treatments you would prescribe in the ongoing management of this\npatient. (4 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img13416761d22dc22c14"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>9/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Posterior tibial nerve block</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Landmarks:</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Posterior to medial malleolus &amp; anterior to Achilles tendon</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Superior by 0.5-1cm from posterior tibial artery</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Also accept Ankle Block if all nerves and landmarks at listed below.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">* Posterior Tibial Nerve Block</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">- Palpate the posterior tibial artery behind the medial malleolus. Inject 3-5-mL of</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">anesthetic 1-cm in depth 1-cm superior to this point.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">* Sural Nerve Block</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">- Inject 3-5-mL of anesthetic subcutaneously in a band between the Achilles tendon and 1-</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">cm above lateral malleolus.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">* Superficial Peroneal Nerve Block</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Inject 4-10-mL of anesthetic subcutaneously in a band between the lateral</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">malleolus and the extensor hallucis longus tendon.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">* Deep Peroneal Nerve Block</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Have the patient dorsiflex their foot and insert the needle 1-cm above the medial</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">malleolus aiming underneath the extensor hallucis longus tendon and advance</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">until you hit the tibia. Then inject 3-5-mL of anesthetic.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">* Saphenous Nerve Block</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></td><td><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></td><td><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></td><td><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Inject 2-5-mL subcutaneously between the medial malleolus and the anterior tibial</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">tendon.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>II.</strong><strong> </strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Xylocaine 1% (Lignocaine) or Bupivocaine (0.25% or 0.5%) up to maximum of 5mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without Adrenaline</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>III.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Primary closure with sutures with 3-0 Nylon, interrupted sutures</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(Wound glue not preferred in dirty wound)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>IV.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Ciprofloxacin 400mg bd oral (prophylaxis for freshwater exposure)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tetanus immunoglobulin 250 IU IMI (as unimmunized)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ADT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Simple analgesia with Paracetamol 15,g/kg or Ibuprofen 10mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":5,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 Marks)\nAn 86 year old man presents with acute pleuritic chest pain and shortness of breath.\nHe is a long term smoker. (His chest x-‐ray is shown in the props booklet)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three (3) radiological abnormalities on his x-‐ray: (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List three (3) possible causes for the x-‐ray findings His vitals are:\n\nHR\n100 bpm\n\nBP\n150/80\n\nRR\n25/min\n\nSats\n86% on 4L O2\n\nTemp\n38.1 degrees"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List and justify three (3) initial interventions: (6 marks) Intervention Reason"}],"propPages":["asset:imgf4c3fcc96710f729b1"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Marked hyperinflation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Moderate/Large left sided pneumothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Mediastinal shift to the right – possible tension PTX</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Bilateral widespread pulmonary infiltrates</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Right midzone (basal segment of upper lobe) wedge shaped cavitating</div><div class=\"answer-line\">lesion/consolidation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Right apical focal consolidation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Interposed hepatic flexure under right hemidiaphragm (Chilaiditti</div><div class=\"answer-line\">syndrome)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\"> Cavitating pneumonia – Klebsiella, S. Aureus, Pneumoccocus, TB</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"> Malignancy – primary bronchogenic CA</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"> Interstitial pneumonitis - Influenza/viral</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"> Pneumothorax – rupture of large bullae</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>His vitals are: </strong></td></tr><tr><td>HR</td><td>100 bpm</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>BP</td><td>150/80</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>RR</td><td>25/min</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Sats</td><td>86% on 4L O2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Temp</td><td>38.1 degrees</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></td><td><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></td><td><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></td><td><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Intervention</td><td>Reason</td></tr><tr><td>Titrate O2 therapy – Hudson/venturi</td><td>Hypoxic with likely underlying</td></tr><tr><td>to maintain sats 88-92%.</td><td>emphysema and hypercarbia</td></tr><tr><td>Broad spectrum antibiotics – Pip/Taz</td><td>Multifocal cavitating consolidation –</td></tr><tr><td>or combination abx that includes</td><td>broad differential organisms:</td></tr><tr><td>anaerobic cover</td><td>Klebsiella, staph, pneumococcus</td></tr><tr><td>Intercostal catheter/pig tail</td><td>Large pneumothorax with mediastinal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">shift. Poor respiratory reserve</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":6,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 Marks) Your director has asked you to take on the ‘Quality Assurance’ portfolio within your department.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Define the following terms in relation to your portfolio. (3 marks) Quality assurance Clinical indicators Benchmarking"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List five (5) commonly used quality measures in Emergency Departments in Australasia. (5 marks) You are to lead a departmental project on a specific indicator within your department."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe the four (4) steps of the quality assurance cycle."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">A system to establish standards of care and to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">monitor how well these standards are met</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Clinical indicators </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Measures of clinical outcomes of care that help to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">point to potential problems and allow comparative</div><div class=\"answer-line\">data</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Benchmarking </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Comparing performance with others to act as a</div><div class=\"answer-line\">marker and goal for improvement</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Waiting times per ATS category</div><div class=\"answer-line\">‘Did not wait’ rates</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Flow measures, e.g. 4 hour ED times</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Time to analgaesia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Morbidity and mortality reviews</div><div class=\"answer-line\">X-ray follow up</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Patient satisfaction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">There are many more…</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Plan – </strong>review relevant literature and data with relevant stakeholders and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">formulate plan.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Do –</strong> implement plan through staff engagement</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Study –</strong> evaluate plan after a pre-defined period of time</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Act –</strong> Adjust plan accordingly as per initial evaluation.  And repeat.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":7,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 Marks)\nA 2 year old boy is brought to a suburban emergency department. The mother reports\nthat the child has been increasingly distressed due to pain after the foreskin was retracted that morning. She has been unable to return the foreskin to its normal position. (His photo is shown in props booklet)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 2 abnormalities in this photograph.\n(2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Describe three (3) methods for the reduction of the foreskin. (6 marks, 2 marks for each method)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe the technique for a penile block. (4 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:imge3ec7488fbbf173b6b"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Paraphymosis (Essential)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Grossly oedematous foreskin</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Plethoric and engorged glans, suggestive of obstructed venous return.  High risk of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ischaemia.  (Essential)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td> Manual traction:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>o Circumferential pressure, to reduce swelling, and distal traction.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>o Consider use of osmotic agents (i.e. sugar) to reduce swelling.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td> Aspiration of paraphymosis:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>o Requires penile block or sedation</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>o Multiple needle pricks to allow drainage of oedematous foreskin.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td> Surgical:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>o Dorsal slit (Cosmetically undesirable)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>o Circumcision.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></td><td><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong> <strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></td><td><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Consent</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Sterile technique</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Local anaesthetic: Bupivicaine 0.25% 0.1ml/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Injection site:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Dorsal nerves of penis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Pull penis downwards</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Inject perpendicular to the skin at 10:30 &amp; 01:30 0.5-1cm lateral to and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">caudal to the pubic symphysis.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Caution with regards to dorsal penile vessels</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ref:  Smith’s anaesthesia for infants and children</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":8,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 Marks)\nA 16 year old girl is brought in by her parents due to concern about her weight and\nmental health.\nShe weighs 38kg.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four (4) features on history that may suggest an eating disorder . (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four (4) medical complications that may occur due to an eating disorder. (4 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What principles of risk assessment need to be considered when deciding her treatment?"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>9/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Intense fear of gaining weight</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Preoccupation with body image / body dysmorphia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Change in eating habits – intense dieting</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Excessive or compulsive exercise</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Significant weight fluctuations</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Obsessive food rituals</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Induced purging after meals/laxative use</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Deceptive/ secretive behaviour around food</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Cardiac – hypotension, heart failure, arrhythmias, cardiomyopathy</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Haematological – anaemia, leukopaenia, thrombocytopaenia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Musculoskeletal – osteopenia/osteoporosis leading to fractures</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Gastrointestinal – gastritis, PUD, oesophagitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Gynaecological – amenorrhoea, infertility</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Neurological – peripheral neuropathy</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Presence of immediate life threats – arrhythmias, signs of heart failure,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hypotension</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Patient’s capacity to consent/refuse treatment</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Likely compliance with treatment</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Active suicide risk</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Family/social/community support</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":9,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 18 marks)\nA 60 year old lady presents to ED with generalised abdominal pain. She was about to have\ndinner when she noticed onset of constant severe pain. This is associated with nausea without vomiting. She denies any change in bowel motions or complaints of melaena. She has been well of late with no fevers, chills, rigors or sweats. She has no complaints of urinary symptoms. She has a past medical history of paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, insulin dependent diabetes mellitus and chronic obstructive airway disease. On clinical examination, she is generally tender with no evidence of peritonism. Bowel sounds were present and normal. Her regular medications include:\nAspirin 100mg PO OD\n• \nLantus 30 units SC NOCTE\n• \nNovorapid 10 -‐ 15 units SC TDS\n• \nSalbutamol INH PRN\n• \nSalmeterol/Fluticasone INH BD\n• \nHer observations are;\n\nTemp\n37.3 degrees\n\nBP\n160/80 mmHg Pulse 110 bpm O2 Sats. 92% RA\n\nRR\n22 breaths/min\n\nGCS\n15/15","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Based on the assessment above, what is the MOST LIKELY diagnosis? (1 mark) An initial abdominal XRAY was performed. Refer to prop booklet. (Image A)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Based on this XRAY, list 2 relevant positive and negatives. (2 marks) Relevant positives (0.5 each) Relevant negatives(0.5 each)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List three (3) other differential diagnoses that should be considered?"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List five (5) important aspects to the management of this patient. (5 marks) A CT abdomen has been performed. Refer to prop booklet (Image B)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"On this axial image, what does the arrow indicate? (1 mark)"},{"id":"f","label":"Part F","prompt":"List 3 other causes of this CT finding.\n(3 marks)"},{"id":"g","label":"Part G","prompt":"Apart from the above CT finding, list 3 other findings on assessment which would indicate poor prognosis. Image B"}],"propPages":["asset:imged9d48fbdffbb4c2de","asset:imgde647ab71e905a09e7"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>12/18</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Mesenteric Ischaemia/Bowel Infarct</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">(2 marks, 0.5 mark each)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Positives - Dilated small bowel loops, Thickened bowel wall with “Thumbprint</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">sign”</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Negatives - No portal venous gas, No pneumatosis intestinalis, No faecal loading</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Needs to be relevant to stem</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Ruptured Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Acute bowel obstruction</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Bowel perforation</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Acute appendicitis</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Renal colic</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Biliary colic</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td></td><td>Supportive care - Analgesia, Keep NBM, IV fluids, NG tube insertion</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Antibiotics - appropriate antibiotic choice</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Heparin infusion</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Early surgical input for laparotomy</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Consideration of interventional approaches - Endovascular stenting, Catheter</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">directed thrombolysis, Intra-arterial vasodilators</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td></td><td>Pneumatosis intestinalis</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></td><td><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></td><td><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></td><td><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part F</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Primary - Benign idiopathic conditions 15% of cases - Submucosal/Subserosal</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">cysts</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Secondary - Infectious enteritis, COPD, Coeliac disease, Leukaemia, AIDs,</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Amyloidosis, Post-Chemotherapy enteritis, Connective tissue disorders</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part G</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td></td><td>Presence of bloody diarrhoea</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Lactic acidosis</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Presence of portal venous gas</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Thickened bowel wall on imaging</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":10,"title":"","stemText":"Booklet two:\nSAQ 10-‐18\nBooklet three: SAQ 19-‐27 Props Booklet: All props\nSAQ 10 (9 Minutes)\n(Total 18 marks)\nA 65 year old man cuts the lateral aspect of his right lower leg on a rusty piece of wire\nwhile gardening. He washes the area with fresh water from his garden shed kettle, and wraps the wound in an old cloth, before seeing his GP the next day.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 5 types of wound that are more likely to favour the growth of tetanus\n(ie. are considered ‘tetanus prone’) (5 Marks) Even though the next day his GP considers the wound to be “just a nasty scratch really” and wipes the area down with disinfectant then changes the dressing, the GP does believe it is ‘tetanus prone’. He gives the man a tetanus toxoid vaccine, but wonders if intramuscular tetanus immunoglobulin (TIG) is also needed?"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 3 indications for giving TIG to a patient in addition to tetanus vaccine.\n(3 marks) Three days later the man returns to his GP concerned that his leg is infected. The GP suspects necrotising fasciitis."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List seven (7) features on history and examination that would support a diagnosis of necrotising fasciitis. (7 Marks) The man is transported urgently to your ED. He looks unwell, and you agree that he has likely necrotising fasciitis."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List three (3) priorities in his immediate management. (3 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>11/18</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Compound fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bite wound</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Deep penetrating wound</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Wound with extensive tissue damage (burn or contusion)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Wound containing a foreign body (espec. wood splinter)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Wound contaminated with soil, dust, manure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Wound where intervention such as surgical debridement is delayed (&gt; 6 hr)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Wound complicated by pyogenic infection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IVDU espec. with dirty needle</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Avulsed tooth</div><div class=\"answer-line\">[ <em><strong>Australian Immunisation Handbook, 10</strong></em><em><strong>th</strong></em><em><strong> edition: 4.19 ‘Tetanus’</strong></em> ]</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">History of tetanus vaccination: &lt; 3 doses or uncertain</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Time since last dose: &gt; 10 years</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Type of wound: all wounds other than ‘clean minor wounds’</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Any individuals with humoral immunodeficiency (eg. HIV) following any tetanus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">prone wound, regardless of prior tetanus vaccination status</div><div class=\"answer-line\">[ <em><strong>Australian Immunisation Handbook, 10</strong></em><em><strong>th</strong></em><em><strong> edition: 4.19 ‘Tetanus’</strong></em> ]</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Underlying risk factor: diabetes, PVD, immunosuppression incl. HIV, recent injury,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">recent surgery, malignancy – ONE mark for risk factor(s)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pain out of proportion to physical findings</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Erythematous skin</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Change in skin colour: red-purple progressing to blue-gray, then bullous (accept</div><div class=\"answer-line\">‘Change in skin’ as an <em>additional</em> mark to erythematous)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Poorly demarcated skin changes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Swelling</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Induration of limb</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Exquisitely tender</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pain on passive movement of involved muscle groups</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Weakness of involved muscle groups</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Paraesthesiae / anaesthesia (late)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Systemic toxicity with sepsis (late)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">[ <em><strong>https://www.uptodate.com/contents/necrotizing-soft-tissue-infections</strong></em> ]</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Immediate surgical referral for exploration and debridement</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Analgesia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intravenous fluids</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Haemodynamic support, including vasopressors (<em>must</em> be going for Surgery</div><div class=\"answer-line\">regardless)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":11,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 Marks)\nA 3-‐year-‐old boy is brought in to your ED by his mother with concerns that he may have\nswallowed a foreign body. The child is sitting on his mother’s lap, alert.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four (4) signs of airway obstruction from a swallowed foreign body that you would look for in this child. (4 marks) (See props booklet for larger scale X-‐ray) A lateral neck x-‐ray is taken and is pictured below."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the relevant features of the x-‐ray. (2 marks) Shortly after your assessment, the child becomes agitated and has a vomit. He subsequently becomes apnoeic and unresponsive."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State your management by filling in the following table. (6 marks) If unsuccessful progress to... If unsuccessful progress to... If unsuccessful progress to..."}],"propPages":["asset:imgeb299a62fdf1dbdbdf"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>10/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Stridor</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Drooling</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Abnormal/hoarse voice or cry</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Respiratory distress</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Wheeze</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Radio-opaque FB (likely coin) seen in oesophagus.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FB is clearly below the laryngeal inlet and not causing obstruction of trachea.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Open Airway.  Remove FB if visible</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":12,"title":"","stemText":"(12 Marks)\nA 74 year old female patient presents to your ED with central chest pain, dyspnoea and\nfeeling lightheaded. This came on 1 hour ago whilst resting on the couch at home. Her background history includes a pacemaker which was inserted for slow AF.\nHer vital signs are:\n\nGCS\n15/15\n\nBP\n85/50\n\nSpO2 94% RA\nHer ECG is shown below: (See props booklet for ECG please)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State the key findings of her ECG and how they may be relevant to her presentation. (4 marks) Diagnosis:"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline your immediate management priorities for this patient in the ED. (6 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What is the role of a magnet in managing pacemaker dysfunction? (2 marks) ."}],"propPages":["asset:img0ba29ef3037ad3df93"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>7</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Paced rhythm at rate 130/min</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pacing spikes after every QRS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Retrograde P waves (Seen best in Leads 1, V1, V2, V5, V6)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Likely pacemaker mediated tachycardia causing rate related ischaemic symptoms</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiac monitoring and defibrillation pads applied</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Supplemental O2 to maintain SPo2 &gt;94%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fluid bolus 250mL N/saline aiming for MAP &lt; 70</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Analgesia – Fentanyl 25-50mcg IV for chest pain</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Apply magnet to pacemaker</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Discussion with Cardiology and interrogation of pacemaker.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">When applied converts to asynchronous pacing which turns of the sensing mode of the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">pacemaker and allows the pacemaker to pace the atria &amp; ventricles asynchronously.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":13,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 marks) You have been approached by a senior clinical nurse in your ED informing you of a problem with a trainee. She is concerned that the trainee has an alcohol problem. On multiple occasions, the trainee has posted about his alcohol use on social media. She is concerned that there may also be other recreational drug use. Several other nurses have reported that he is abrupt, rude and does not communicate his plans. This registrar is an early phase advanced trainee who recently completed his primary examinations.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 examples of conduct that would fulfil Mandatory Reporting\nrequirements to the Medical Board. (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"You have organised to meet with this trainee to further discuss this issue.\nList 5 main points you would cover in this meeting.\n(5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Following this meeting, you have identified that your department does not have a guideline on mandatory reporting. List six (6) important aspects required when developing a guideline. (3 marks, 0.5 each)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td></td><td>Practising while intoxicated on alcohol or drugs</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Sexual misconduct in the practice of the profession</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Placing the public at substantial harm because of an impairment / health issue</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Placing the public at risk because of a significant departure from accepted</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">professional standards</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td></td><td>Investigate and ensure no need for mandatory reporting</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Identify work stressors</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Identify personal stressors</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Identify lifestyle factors - drug or alcohol use</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Identify and offer ongoing support - Family/Friends/Mentor/DEMTs</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Offer alcohol/drug liaison service referral</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Offer time off / roster changes to accommodate for trainee burnout</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></td><td><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></td><td><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></td><td><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Gather information</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">o Benchmarking</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">o Evidenced based guidelines</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">o Guidelines from other hospitals</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Identify and involve all relevant stakeholders</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Set objective and time frame</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Develop draft guideline and circulate for comment</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Implementation</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">o Regular education and guideline promotion until targets achieved</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Post-implementation evaluation and adjustment</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">o Regular guideline review</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":14,"title":"","stemText":"(Total marks 12) You are the ED consultant on duty in an urban district hospital with obstetric and paediatric services available.\nA 29 year old pregnant female who is 32 weeks pregnant (G1 P0) presents to your\ndepartment with a 2 day history of headache, blurry vision, lethargy and upper abdominal\npain. She has so far had an uncomplicated pregnancy and has been receiving antenatal care in your hospital. The patient’s vitals signs on arrival are as follows\n\nHR\n105 per minute\n\nBP\n150 / 95 mmHg\n\nRR\n20 per minute\nOxygen Sats 97% on room air\nOne of your registrars has already picked up the patient and arranged for some bloods and the patient’s early blood results are as follow: Parameter Patient Value Normal Adult Value\n\nHb\n76 G/L (115 - 160)\n\nPlatelets\n65 x 109 / L (140 - 400)\n\nINR\n2.1 (0.9 - 1.3)\n\nAPTT\n62 seconds (25 -‐38) LDH 704 U/L (110 - 250) Total Bilirubin 58 micromol/L ( < 20)\n\nUrea\n28 mmol/L (3 - 8)\n\nCreatinine\n324 micromol/L (70 - 120) Potassium 5.0 mmol/L (3.2 - 4.5)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four (4) most likely differential diagnoses. (4 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four (4) more investigations you think are warranted for this patient and state the clinical reasoning. Investigation (0.5 Mark for each) Clinical Reasoning (0.5 Mark for each) After you have reviewed the blood results with your registrar you head promptly to review the patient and to inform her of the blood results. As you enter the cubicle the patient has a witnessed generalised tonic clonic seizure. The patient is moved to resus cubicle for full non-‐invasive monitoring during this process:"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List four (4) immediate clinical response to this situation. (4 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Model answers:</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Pre-eclampsia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> HELLP Syndrome</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Sepsis with DIC</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> HUS – TTP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Acute fatty liver of pregnancy</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Model Answer</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Investigation  (0.5 mark for each)</td><td>Clinical Reasoning (0.5 Mark for each)</td></tr><tr><td>Urinalysis</td><td>Proteinuria, WBC, WCC, Casts</td></tr><tr><td>Full set LFTs</td><td>Assessment of HELLP syndrome</td></tr><tr><td>Peripheral Blood Smear</td><td>Evidence of haemolysis</td></tr><tr><td>Haemolysis Screen</td><td>Reticulocyte Count, Haptoglobins,</td></tr><tr><td>Conjugated / Unconjugated Bilirubin</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>for causes of haemolysis and anaemia</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Full Septic Screen</td><td>Sepsis as potential cause for DIC: Blood</td></tr><tr><td>cultures / Urine mcs / Sputum /</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Vaginal swabs</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>USS Liver</td><td>Seeking haematoma / rupture</td></tr><tr><td><strong>After you have reviewed the blood results with your registrar you head promptly to </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>review the patient and to inform her of the blood results.  </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>As you enter the cubicle the patient has a witnessed generalised tonic clonic </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>seizure. The patient is moved to resus cubicle for full non-invasive monitoring </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>during this process: </strong></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Model answers:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Prevent maternal hypoxia, left lateral position to prevent aortocaval compression + O2 as</div><div class=\"answer-line\">required to maintain sats &gt; 92%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seizure termination: MgSO4 4g over 5 minutes followed by infusion 1g/hr for 24 hours.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Management of maternal hypertension</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Hydralazine 10 – 20 mg IV slowly</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Beta blockers: Labetolol 5 – 10 mg IV slowly (also accept metoprolol,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">esomolol)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o GTN / SNP also accepted</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Escalation for prompt delivery of baby: Urgent obstetric attendance and arrangement for</div><div class=\"answer-line\">prompt delivery.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Steroid therapy for fetal lung maturation: Betamethasone 12mg IM</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> References: Tintinalli 7th Edition: Chapter 104 Emergencies after 20 weeks of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">pregnancy and postpartum period, Page 696 - 698</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false,"investigationTables":[{"id":"investigation-1","title":"Blood results","headers":["Parameter","Patient V alue","Normal Adult Value"],"rows":[["Hb","76 G/L","(115 – 160)"],["Platelets","65 x 109 / L","(140 – 400)"],["INR","2.1","(0.9 – 1.3)"],["APTT","62 seconds","(25 -‐38)"],["LDH","704 U/L","(110 – 250)"],["Total B ilirubin","58 micromol/L","( < 2 0)"],["Urea","28 mmol/L","(3 – 8)"],["Creatinine","324 micromol/L","(70 – 120)"],["Potassium","5.0 mmol/L","(3.2 – 4.5)"]]}]},{"number":15,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 12 Marks) Your local wilderness agency is requesting assistance in review of their medical protocols for a mountaineering expedition. They need to provide a brochure to give to potential clients who wish to trek up the nominated mountain (6000m).","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four (4) risk factors for Acute Mountain Sickness (AMS) (4 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four (4) symptoms of High Altitude Cerebral Oedema (HACE) (4 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List four (4) treatment options required for patients with HACE (4 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Previous AMS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Rapid ascent</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Higher altitudes &gt;3000m</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Strenuous physical exertion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Younger patients</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Headache</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Confusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Ataxia </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Drowsiness</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Stupor</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Coma</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Descent</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Supplemental O2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Portable HBO</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Acetazolamide 250mg po BD</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Dexamethasone 8mg iv initially, then 4mg qid iv/po/im</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":16,"title":"","stemText":"(Total marks 12)\nA 40 year old woman was just intubated in your department following a severe\nexacerbation of her asthma. She weighs approximately 60kg. Your registrar comes to you seeking advice as the ventilator keeps alarming. The patient’s vital signs are:\n\nHR\n120 bpm\n\nBP\n90/60 mmHg\n\nSats\n100% You find the ventilator settings below Tidal Volume 600ml Pressure support PEEP\n\nFiO2\n100% Rate","parts":[{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Which ventilator settings would you alter from the above settings? (4 marks) Ventilator settings Alterations"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List two (2) other steps you could perform to optimize her ventilation (2 marks) The patient becomes hypotensive with a BP 80/50 mmHg ten minutes after intubation"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"What is your immediate action? (1 mark)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List five (5) possible reasons for the hypotension (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"removedNonQuestionResponseFragments":["E ratio 1:2"],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Tidal Volume</td><td>Aim 6-8ml/kg = 360-480 ml</td></tr><tr><td>PEEP</td><td>Decrease to 5 or 0</td></tr><tr><td>FiO2</td><td>Titrate down to maintain sats &gt; 90% or pO2</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">&gt; 60mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Rate</td><td>Reduce to 8-10 breaths/min</td></tr><tr><td>I:E ratio</td><td>Increase 1:4-6</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>II.</strong><strong> </strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Pass mark (8/12) </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Disconnect ETT from ventilator and hand ventilate with BVM</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>IV.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Oversedation/induction drugs</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Anaphylaxis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Tension pneumothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Dynamic hyperinflation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Arrhythmias</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Hypovolemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":17,"title":"","stemText":"(Total Marks 12)\nA 28 year old office worker presents by ambulance following a conscious collapse at\nwork. She had been unwell at work that day. On arrival, her vital signs are:\n\nHR\n160 -‐190\n\nBP\n190/110\n\nTemp 39.5\n\nRR\n\nSats\n92% on RA\n\nGCS\n14/15\n\nBSL\n11.6 She is confused, tremulous and anxious. There is mild pitting oedema","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List five (5) differential diagnoses other than Thyroid storm? (2.5 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"The husband arrives and reports a recent history of a neck lump which is due to be investigated. Closer examination reveals a diffuse multi-‐nodular goitre. What five (5) underlying conditions can cause thyrotoxicosis? (2.5 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What are three (3) clinical findings with thyrotoxicosis? (3 Marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List four (4) immediate treatment priorities with drug names and doses? (4 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>-</td><td>CNS infection</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Heat stroke</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Sympathomimetic overdose</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Serotonin syndrome</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Phaeochromocytoma</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>-</td><td>Graves’ disease</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Toxic multinodular goitre</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Toxic adenoma</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></td><td><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></td><td><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></td><td><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Thyroiditis</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Drug induced e.g. amiodarone, lithium iodine</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Postpartum</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Temperature &gt;37.8</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tachycardia out of proportion to fever</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CNS disturbance</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>-</td><td>Supportive including sedation and active cooling</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Management of cardiac complications with nonselective beta-blocker –</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">propranolol</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o 1mg Q5min to max 10mg</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Prevention of peripheral conversion of T4-T3 with corticosteroids</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Decreased production with propylthiouracil (900-1200mg)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Referral for definitive management of underlying disease</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":18,"title":"","stemText":"(Total 18 Marks) You are the consultant working in an urban district ED. The ambulance bring in a 14 year\n• ld girl on an involuntary order after her mother found her in the bathroom with some\nempty pill packets.\nShe weighs 40kg.\n\nObservations:\n\nHR\n105\n\nRR\n\nSats\n97% RA\n\nBP\n105/60\n\nGCS\n13/15 E3V4M6 The ambulance also bring in some empty pill packets, which were all prescribed yesterday. The missing pills include: 20 x 20mg Citalopram 20 x 300mg Quetiapine 50 x 25mg Amitriptylline 24 x 500mg/30mg Paracetamol/Codeine","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four (4) key aspects of your risk assessment based on what she has taken? (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"For the table below, List three (3) key investigations that you would perform to help refine your risk assessment, including your"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What specific toxidrome would you assess this patient for? (1 mark)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List four (4) clinical features consistent with this toxidrome (4 marks) Further information She clinically deteriorates, with GCS now 9/15 and BP 80/40"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List four (4) key treatments you would provide, including details: (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>12/18</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Taken multiple serotonergic medications – risk of serotonin syndrome</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Multiple medications that may cause sedation – ALOC/need for airway protection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. tCA overdose &gt;30mg/kg – associated with severe toxicity</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Paracetamol potential toxic dose ie &gt;10g, or &gt;150mg/kg</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ECG – signs of sodium channel blockade or QT prolongation in context tCA and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">SSRI/antipsychotics – QRS broadening, R prime in aVR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">VBG – metabolic acidosis and/or resp acidosis in setting ALOC</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Paracetamol level – help determine need for NAC in this potentially toxic ingestion</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Serotonin syndrome</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Mental Status Changes </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Delirium </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Psychomotor agitation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Autonomic stimulation </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Diarrhoea </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Flushing</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypertension</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hyperthermia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Mydriasis </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Sweating  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Tachycardia </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Neuromuscular excitation </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Clonus – especially ankle/ocular </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Hyperreflexia </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Increased tone – lower limbs&gt;upper </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Rigidity</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bold points are those signicantly associated</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ideally candidates would give at least one sign from each of the 3 areas</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Clonus/hyper-reflexia/increased tone/rigidity – probably all count as one point ie almost</div><div class=\"answer-line\">similes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Same as</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hypertension/tachycardia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sweating/flushing</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Intubate, hyperventilate aiming for pH&gt;7.5</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Fluid bolus 20ml/kg 0.9% NaCl</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Sodium bicarbonate 100mmol IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Activated charcoal 50g via NGT post intubation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Would also accept inotropes if given as answer</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and The Royal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":19,"title":"","stemText":"Total 18 Marks\nA 77 year old male presents with lower back pain after a recent minor trip. He has a\nnormal lower limb neurological examination, and no focal spinal tenderness. He is noted to have bruising on his buttocks, and routine biochemical examination is taken and is as follows:\n\nHb 60\n\nWCC 0.4\nPlt 6\nMCV 81\nHCT 0.17\nNeutrophils 0.18","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe the relevant features of these blood tests: (1 Mark)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List three central and three peripheral causes which must be considered as an underlying diagnosis. (3 Marks) Causes: Central (0.5 each) Peripheral (0.5 each)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"He will require transfusion of blood products. List four (4) blood products which may be used (carefully) without confirming ABO compatibility? (4 Marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"He is transfused 2 units and becomes acutely hypoxic, and dyspnoeic.\nPlease complete the following table with respect to transfusion reactions: (10 Marks) Type of reaction Incidence Two (2)Discriminating (1 mark each) (0.5 mark each) features on examination or investigation (0.5 Mark each)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Pancytopenia – all cell lines affected – with critical anaemia and thrombocytopenia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>II.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Empty Marrow: aplastic anaemia,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Auto-immune</div><div class=\"answer-line\">myelofibrosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Severe sepsis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Infiltration by abnormal cells:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">leukaemia, lymphoma, solid tumours,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">TB</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Deranged marrow: MDS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Drug induced: chemotherapy, sulpha</div><div class=\"answer-line\">drugs, alcohol</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>III.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. FFP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">d. Prothrombinex</div><div class=\"answer-line\">e. Cryoprecipitate</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>IV.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Anaphylaxis/allergy</td><td>Anaphylaxis 1:20,000-</td><td>Urticaria</td></tr><tr><td>1:50,000</td><td>Bronchospasm</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>(very rare)</td><td>Abdominal cramps</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Milder allergy &lt;1-3%</td><td>Low grade fever</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Acute haemolytic</td><td>1:75,000</td><td>Haemoglobinuria</td></tr><tr><td>reaction</td><td>(rare)</td><td>Flank pain</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">DIC</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Shock</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Acute renal failure</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Positive COOMBs test</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Reduced haptoglobins</td></tr><tr><td>Febrile non-</td><td>0.1-1%</td><td>Usually isolated fever</td></tr><tr><td>haemolytic reaction</td><td>(common)</td><td>Absence of any other cause found</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Normal investigations</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>TACO (transfusion</td><td>&lt;1%</td><td>Clinical signs of heart failure</td></tr><tr><td>related circulatory</td><td>CXR suggestive of APO</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>overload)</td><td>Raised BNP</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Bacteraemic shower</td><td>1:75,000 for platelets;</td><td>Rigors</td></tr><tr><td>1:500,000 for RBCs</td><td>High fever</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Positive culture from blood</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">product and patient</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Note marks will not be given for TRALI – this is a delayed reaction and would not be</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">expected to onset &lt;24hrs.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":20,"title":"","stemText":"(12 Marks)\nA 28 year old male patient is brought to your ED following an injury in a tackle during a\nrugby match. There is concern for a C spine injury as he been unable to move his upper or lower limbs after the injury. On arrival he is being managed in appropriate spinal immobilisation. His vitals signs are:\n\nGCS\n15/15\n\nTemp 36.0\n\nHR\n60 regular\n\nBP\n90/60\n\nSpO2 93% RA\nQuestions:","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the following table with 4 indications for intubation that may occur in a patient with a spinal injury and state the supporting features you would look for on assessment. (8 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 modifications would you make to your standard RSI in this patient if you\nwere to proceed with intubation in the ED?"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>9/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Model answers: </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Any of the following acceptable:</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Model answers:</strong>  Any of the following for 1 mark each. Only 1 mark for intubation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">technique as candidate needs to mention other parameters.<strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Preload with fluid prior to induction to prevent hypotension (eg N/saline</div><div class=\"answer-line\">500mL bolus) and prepare push dose pressors (eg Metaraminol,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ephedrine) to maintain MAP &gt;70</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pretreat with Atropine 600mcg to minimise bradycardia with intubation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Inductions drugs to ensure haemodynamic stability (eg Ketamine</div><div class=\"answer-line\">102mg/kg)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Use of Video laryngoscope to minimise neck movements during</div><div class=\"answer-line\">intubation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Anticipate difficult view on laryngoscopy and prepare adjuncts such as</div><div class=\"answer-line\">bougie, VL</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Consider awake fibreoptic intubation in theatre if necessary resources &amp;</div><div class=\"answer-line\">expertise available</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Maintain manual inline stabilisation during intubation to minimise neck</div><div class=\"answer-line\">movement<strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">N.B Suxamethonium is not contraindicated in first 72 hrs after spinal injury so no mark</div><div class=\"answer-line\">given for this answer<strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":21,"title":"","stemText":"(Total Marks 12)\nA 50 year-‐old woman is in your ED with epigastric pain and vomiting. She has a\nbackground of hypertension and GORD. You are reviewing her on the ward round as an ECG is being performed. She states that she feels a bit light headed. The following ECG is recorded: (See prop booklet for her ECG please)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three (3) relevant abnormalities on her ECG. (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State 3 possible causes for this clinical presentation.\n(3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patient’s BP is 100/60 mmHg. List three (3) potential treatment, including doses and endpoints where relevant. (6 marks) Drug and dose End point"}],"propPages":["asset:img115ebcc1b8f55c3d10"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>9/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Extreme sinus Bradycardia (rate approx. 20 bpm)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Likely prolonged QTc</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ventricular ectopic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">‘R on T’ phenomenon causing Torsades</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>II.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Severe hypokalaemia/hypomagnesaemia secondary to vomiting</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Use of QTc prolonging medication relevant to clinical scenario – anti-emetics, eg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">metoclopramide, ondansetron</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Myocardial ischaemia, with epigastric pain an ACS equivalent presentation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Note:</strong> The above causes should be relevant to the clinical scenario, hence the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">wording of the question.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>III.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">IV magnesium (10 mmol or 2g) over 1-2 minutes to termination of rhythm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV potassium if associated hypokalaemia 10-20 mmol IV over 1 hour to K+ &gt;3.5</div><div class=\"answer-line\">mmol</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Overdrive cardiac pacing to rate of 90-120 bpm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Isoprenaline infusion 0.05-1 microg/kg/min to rate of 90-120 bpm</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":22,"title":"","stemText":"(Total Marks 12)\nA 32 year old male is brought to your ED by the ambulance in refractory VF. He has\na down time of approximately 35minutes and has received advanced life support in accordance with published guidelines. His partner states he self discharged yesterday after being diagnosed with myocarditis.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"The Australian and New Zealand Committee on Resuscitation recommend the use\n• f prospectively validated termination of resuscitation (TOR) rules for out of\nhospital cardia arrests (OHCA).\nList 4 criteria used by a prospectively validated TOR rule in the setting of OHCA\nreceiving advanced life support: (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List two (2) pros and two (2) cons with respect to the use of mechanical CPR devices in OHCA (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Which Extra Corporeal Membrane Oxygenation (ECMO) modality would be most appropriate for this patient and why? (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List two (2) contraindications to commencement of ECMO. (2 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>7/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">The Australian and New Zealand Committee on Resuscitation recommend the use</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">of prospectively validated termination of resuscitation (TOR) rules for out of</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">hospital cardia arrests (OHCA).</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>List 4 criteria used by a prospectively validated TOR rule in the setting of OHCA </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>receiving advanced life support:  </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>(4 marks need bold to get full marks) </strong></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>No ROSC has occurred</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><strong>No Shock has been administered</strong></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>The OHCA was not witnessed by EMS</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><strong>No bystander CPR performed</strong></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>The OHCA was not witnessed by bystanders</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>No mortality benefit</strong></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>No ROSC benefit</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>No positive neuro outcome benefit</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>May be useful were manual CPR is difficult eg moving vehicle, cath lab etc</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Maybe usefule for prolonged CPR</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>When ECMO CPR is being performed</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Veno-Arterial ECMO</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></td><td><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></td><td><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></td><td><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Heart failure and needs cardiac support.  VV ECMO most suitable for respiratory</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">failure and provides no cardiac support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>Futile (bridge to nowhere) </strong></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Inappropriate use of resources</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Poor baseline level of function</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Terminal illness</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":23,"title":"","stemText":"(Total marks 12)\nA 32 year old female self presents to your emergency department following a fall down 6\nstairs at work.\nShe is 28/40 G1P0.\nShe has no complaints of pain but wants to be ‘checked out’.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What are four of the major cardiovascular physiological changes in pregnancy that may impact on the clinical assessment of this patient? (4 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"On assessment, her primary and secondary survey is normal. List one (1) specific examination and three (3) investigations related to the pregnancy that need to be performed for this patient? (4 Marks) Examination:\n\nInvestigations:\n1:"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What four criteria need to be met for safe discharge of this patient?"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Heart rate increased by 15-20bpm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiac output increased by 40%, gravid uterus pressure on IVC significant</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Uterine blood flow 10% cardiac output</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Also: SVR decreased, arterial BP decreased by 15mmHg, decreased venous return by</div><div class=\"answer-line\">gravid uterus on IVC, increased concentrations of most clotting factors</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>II.</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>On assessment, her primary and secondary survey is normal. List one (1) </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Uterine examination- fundal height, tenderness, contractions</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CTG- role debatable between 24-28/40 but early sensitivity for abnormal fetal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">HR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ultrasound- useful as FAST, examination of solid organ injury, confirm fetal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">presentation and gestation, placental location and amniotic fluid volume. Not a</div><div class=\"answer-line\">good predictor of placental injury.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Blood test- Kleihauer, to determine amount of Anti-D required for fetomaternal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">haemorrhage</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>III.</strong><strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal examination with no abdominal tenderness, bruising or contractions</div><div class=\"answer-line\">No PV loss or discharge</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal fetal movements</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Safe discharge environment- s/work, explored DV, increased antenatal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">surveillance</div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":24,"title":"","stemText":"Total Marks 12\nA 60 year old man is brought by ambulance to your tertiary ED following a ‘one punch’\nattack, where he was punched and fell, hitting his head. There was a brief (<5 minutes) loss of consciousness. He has no prior medical history and is on no medications. On arrival his vital signs are:\n\nGCS\n14/15 (E3, V5, M6) Pupils equal and reactive P\n\nBP\n125/85\n\nRR\nO2 sats 98% room air He has no injuries other than bruising and a large haematoma to the right parietal region\n• f his head. He has a collar in place.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"A single slice from a CT of his head is shown in the props booklet. List four (4) key findings? (4 Marks) On return from CT he is poorly responsive. His GCS is now 7 (E1 V2 M4). His other vital signs do not change. He has an endotracheal tube placed for airway protection and commenced on ventilation without incident. The neurosurgical service is called to attend."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List six (6) priorities in his care while waiting for neurosurgery, (6 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"His pupils are noted to be different sizes left 3mm, right 7mm. List (2) additional interventions that you will perform? (2 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>9/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Right parietal extradural haematoma (can describe bi-convex density and other features</div><div class=\"answer-line\">but need to say it is an EDH)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Air within EDH</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Overlying scalp haematoma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sub-arachnoid haematoma left frontal, likely contre-coup injury</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Mass effect</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Effacement of sulci right parietal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">No appreciable mid-line shift</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Avoid hypotension, maintain cerebral perfusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- aim MAP 70, using cautious fluid bolus 500ml NSaline, escalate to noradrenaline 5-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">20mcg/minute</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Encourage cerebral venous drainage</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- head up 30deg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- loosen cervical collar</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- loosen ETT ties</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ventilation settings</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- avoid hypoxia, titrate FiO2 for Sats &gt;96%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- aim pCO2 35</div><div class=\"answer-line\">General care of ventilated patient</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- IDC and orogastric tube (not NGT)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- analgesia and sedation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Repeat neurological exam</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- pupils</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Osmotherapy - with 500ml 3% Saline IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Facilitate urgent transfer to OT for neurosurgical intervention</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Continue above measures</div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":25,"title":"","stemText":"(Total marks 12) You are the ED Consultant on the day shift at a district general hospital.\nA 61 year old man presents with an injury after a fall while pushing his grandchildren\ndown a slide. After the primary and secondary survey, you determine that his only injury of significance is to his left shoulder. You order x-‐rays of this joint.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List two (2) relevant positives and two (2) relevant negative findings on the above x-‐ray views. Positive: Negative:"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"He has received Methoxyflurane from the ambulance officers, but is still in considerable pain. List four (4) options for acute pain management of this injury. (4 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Having seen the X-‐rays, you determine the need for reduction. Name and briefly describe two techniques for reduction."}],"propPages":["asset:img19701ee08faa95b3ea","asset:img4e7c9f8f8813e5f7f0"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer:</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> 1 mark for each.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Left infra-glenoid shoulder dislocation.*</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Hill-Sachs lesion.*</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Nil Bankart lesion.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Nil AC-joint dislocation.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Nil rib fracture.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Nil subcutaneous emphysema.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Nil pneumothorax.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> * expected answers.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer:</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Analgesic + route + dose considered as one fact/mark.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Titrated intravenous opioid (Fentanyl 25-100mcg, Morphine 2.5-10mg).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Analgesic dose intravenous Ketamine (10-20mg).</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> NSAID (Ketorolac 30mg imi/ivi, Indomethacin 100mg per rectal).</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Nerve block e.g. Supraclavicular Block or Intra-capsular Block with</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bupivacaine 2mg/kg +/- USS-guidance.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Nitrous Oxygen inhaled.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer:</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> 1 mark for name of technique and 1 mark for brief description.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Kocher’s; arm flexed and ad-ducted, gentle traction, external rotation until</div><div class=\"answer-line\">resistance, then extend to saggital plane, then internal rotation to opposite</div><div class=\"answer-line\">shoulder.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Spaso; Extend arm in saggital plan, gentle traction, external +/- internal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">rotation.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Scapular manipulation; patient sitting/prone with arm extended and gentle</div><div class=\"answer-line\">traction, move tip of scapular medially/towards spine.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Milch; ab-duction and external rotation to overhead position with fixation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">of head of humerus.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Stimson; Patient prone with gentle traction/weight to arm.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Cunningham’s; support patient’s arm flexed 90 degrees &amp; ad-ducted, gentle</div><div class=\"answer-line\">traction, massage Trapezius/Deltoid/Biceps sequentially.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Hippocrates; gentle, sustained traction of ab-ducted and flexed arm, with</div><div class=\"answer-line\">counter-traction with sheet.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":26,"title":"","stemText":"(Total Marks 12)\nA 22 year old HIV negative male presents to an urban district emergency department with\na history of unprotected anal intercourse with an anonymous male partner the previous night. He is asymptomatic and requesting post-‐exposure prophylaxis (PEP).","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List (4) other factors on assessment that are important in determining his risk of contracting HIV in this setting? (4marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List (6) important principles of discharge advice that you will provide to the patient. (6 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What other condition may require urgent treatment in this setting and what will determine if this is required? (2 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>6/12</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>-</td><td>Receptive or insertive intercourse (higher risk if receptive)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Presence of ejaculation or withdrawal if receptive  (higher risk if ejaculation)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Circumcision status of patient if insertive (lowers risk, may consider not giving</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">PEP if insertive and circumcised)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Presence of concurrent STI (increases risk)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Trauma during intercourse  (increases risk)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. PEP is not 100% effective</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. The importance of compliance with the treatment regime</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Requires further HIV testing to confirm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Emphasize behaviours to prevent re-exposure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Attend clinic appointment and provide contact details of sexual health clinic to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">which patient is referred.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6. Starter pack is not a full treatment course, this will be provided at follow up</div><div class=\"answer-line\">7. Needs to seek medical advice if feeling unwell as potential for adverse drug</div><div class=\"answer-line\">reactions.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Hepatitis B </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>If unimmunised the patient requires hepatitis B immunisation </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>No marks for immunoglobulin as this is only indicated if the source </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>individual was known to  be infective.  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Source:  Australian national guidelines Post Exposure Prophylaxis 2016.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":27,"title":"","stemText":"(18 marks)\nA 5 day old baby girl is brought into the emergency department. She has had poor feeding\nfor the last 24 hrs and has had increasing lethargy. The baby appears pale, floppy and\nmottled.\nThe child is 3.8kg.\nInitial vital signs are:\n\nHR\n190\n\nRR\n\nCap refill\n5secs\n\nBP\nUnable to record\n\nSats\nNot reading","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List eight (8) differential diagnoses for this presentation (8 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four (4) immediate actions you would take (4 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"You decide to intubate the baby. (6 Marks) List six (6) pieces of equipment (including sizes -multiple sizes do not count as separate answers) that you would prepare for the intubation of this child. Good luck Oh forgot, still you have SCQ in the afternoon J"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>11/18</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Cardiac (Duct dependant lesion)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Sepsis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Electrolyte disturbance</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Metabolic/Endocrine - CAH</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Formula dilution or overconcentration</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Inborn errors of metabolism</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Intestinal catastrophe</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Toxins</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Seizures</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Trauma (Accidental or non accidental)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Oxygen 15L non-rebreather</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> IV/IO/UV access</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Fluid bolus – 10-20ml/kg bolus</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> IV Cefotaxime + ampicillin or reasonable alternative</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Check Blood sugar level</div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Prince Charles Hospital </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>The Royal Brisbane &amp;Women Hospital            </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Redcliffe  and Caboolture  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Emergency Departments</strong><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>     Emergency Department </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>              Emergency Department                               </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Bag/valve mask/neopuff/anaesthetic circuit</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> ETT size 3.5 cuffed (Accept 3 and 4)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Paedcap/capnography</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Laryngoscope size 1 &amp;2 miller and/or mc blade</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Ventilator</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Suction catheter</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Oropharyngeal/nasopharyngeal airways</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Stylet/bougie</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> LMA (Size 1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">published without permission of the DEMT teams. property of The Prince Charles Hospital and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The Royal Brisbane &amp; Women Hospital Emergency</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false}]);
